====================================================================
| Traversing Arrays (for Loop, Enhanced for-each Loop, User Input) |
====================================================================
👉 Traversing an array means accessing each element of the array one by one.
    Index :  0   1   2   3   4
    Value : 10  20  30  40  50

Traversal Order 10 → 20 → 30 → 40 → 50  

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] numbers = {10,20,30,40,50};
        for(int i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++)
        {
            System.out.println(numbers[i]);
            System.out.println("Index = " + i + " Value = " + numbers[i]);
        }
    }
}
------------------------------------------------

int[] numbers = {10,20,30,40,50};          // Traversing in Reverse Order
for(int i = numbers.length - 1; i >= 0; i--)
{
    System.out.println(numbers[i]);
}

--------------------------
| Enhanced for-each Loop |
--------------------------
👉 The enhanced for loop is used to iterate through all elements of an array without using an index.

    for(dataType variable : array)
    {
        // code
    }

    int[] numbers = {10,20,30,40,50};
    for(int num : numbers)
    {
        System.out.println(num);
    }

---------------------------------------
| Difference Between for and for-each |
---------------------------------------
_______________________________________________________________
| `for` Loop                      | `for-each` Loop           |
| ------------------------------- | ------------------------- |
| Uses index                      | No index                  |
| Can modify elements using index | Mainly for reading values |
| More flexible                   | Easier to write           |
| Best for all tasks              | Best for simple traversal |
---------------------------------------------------------------

import java.util.Scanner;
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int[] numbers = new int[5];
        System.out.println("Enter 5 Numbers");
        for(int i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++)
        {
            numbers[i] = sc.nextInt();
        }
        System.out.println("Stored Values");
        for(int i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++)
        {
            System.out.println(numbers[i]);
        }
        sc.close();
    }
}
------------------------------------------------------

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] marks = {85,90,78,95,88};                  // Student Marks
        for(int i = 0; i < marks.length; i++)
        {
            System.out.println("Student " + (i + 1) + " Marks = " + marks[i]);
        }
    }
}

  👉 Traversing means visiting every element one by one.
  👉 for loop is preferred when index is required.
  👉 for-each loop is preferred for read-only traversal.
  👉 Always use arr.length instead of hardcoding the size.
  👉 Reverse traversal starts from arr.length - 1.

